<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ignaz Semmelweis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1818–1865</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ignaz Semmelweis was a Hungarian physician who, working at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-general-hospital">Vienna General Hospital</a> in 1847, showed that childbed fever was greatly reduced when doctors washed their hands in chlorinated lime before examining women in labour. His findings were widely rejected in his lifetime, but they were vindicated by the germ theory of disease, and he is honoured as a pioneer of antiseptic practice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ignaz-semmelweis.jpg" alt="Ignaz Semmelweis" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: József Borsos / Albert Doctor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borsos_%26_Doctor_Semmelweis_Ign%C3%A1c_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
